# The one wildcard in this file, and the exception that proves the rule. A player URL is
# /wpbl/players/<slug>, and the set of valid slugs lives in the database, so it cannot be
# enumerated here. The soft-404 hole it would otherwise open is closed one layer up instead:
# functions/wpbl/[[tab]].ts resolves the slug against the roster and answers a real 404 for
# anything that names nobody, BEFORE this rule is ever reached. Delete that check and every
# typo under /wpbl/players/ silently becomes an indexable page again.
/wpbl/players/*  /  200
# The second wildcard, and the same exception for the same reason: a game URL is
# /wpbl/games/<date>-<away>-at-<home>, and the set of valid slugs is the schedule, which
# lives in the database. functions/wpbl/[[tab]].ts resolves it and answers a real 404 for
# anything naming no game, BEFORE this rule is reached. Note there is deliberately no
# /wpbl/games line above: there is no games index, so the bare path falls through to 404.
/wpbl/games/*    /  200
# The third wildcard, and the same exception again: a comparison is
# /wpbl/compare/<a-slug>-vs-<b-slug>, and the set of valid pairs is the roster squared.
# functions/wpbl/[[tab]].ts resolves both halves and answers a real 404 for anything naming
# fewer than two distinct players, BEFORE this rule is reached. It also 301s the reversed
# order onto the alphabetical one, so a pair is one URL rather than two.
/wpbl/compare/*  /  200
/mlb           /  200
