# Everything else falls through to public/404.html, WITH A 404 STATUS, and there is
# deliberately no rule for it here.
#
# There used to be: `/*  /404.html  404`. It worked, and then it stopped being deployable.
# Cloudflare rejects the whole file at upload time with "Valid status codes are 200, 301,
# 302, 303, 307, or 308. Got 404", which fails the build and leaves the previous deploy
# serving, so the site does not break, it just silently stops updating.
#
# It is not needed anyway. Serving 404.html for an unmatched path is what the platform does
# by default once that file exists, and the reason every unknown URL used to answer 200 with
# the app shell was simply that the file did not exist yet. The rules above are what make
# the app's own routes the exception.
#
# Do not re-add a 404 line here to make the intent explicit. It is not more explicit, it is
# undeployable.
